<Base title="Reading a Manuscript Witness" description="A French and Portuguese manuscript workshop on diplomatic transcription, normalization, translation, folio references, paint layers, and an accountable critical apparatus.">
  <PageIntro eyebrow="Manuscript workshop · Advanced undergraduate / graduate" title="The hand, the caption, and you." description="A clean modern reading hides a series of decisions. Learn to make those decisions visible before you build a historical argument on them."/>
  <section class="section"><article class="wrap manuscript-lesson">
    <nav aria-label="Workshop contents"><strong>In this workshop</strong><a href="#locating">Locate the witness</a><a href="#french">Read the French scrolls</a><a href="#layers">Separate paint layers</a><a href="#portuguese">Test the Portuguese caption</a><a href="#apparatus">Build the apparatus</a><a href="#assignment">Submit an accountable edition</a></nav>
    <div class="lesson-prose">
      <p class="notice-box">Original editorial teaching draft. The linked institutional records support the object metadata and reference readings. These exercises are not a reviewed critical edition. The Getty photograph has been inspected; the Portuguese locator and modern caption have been verified, while the manuscript hand remains a student research task.</p>
      <h2 id="locating">Locate the witness before reading the words</h2>
      <p>“The manuscript says” sounds reassuring. It also leaves out almost everything another reader needs to check. Which manuscript? Which leaf? Which side? Did you consult the book, a photograph, a modern transcription, or a caption beneath a photograph? Each may preserve useful information, but each reaches you through a different chain of decisions.</p>
      <p>Our French exercise uses an illuminated leaf from the <em>Hours of Simon de Varie</em>, held at the Getty as Ms. 7 (85.ML.27), folio 2. The museum calls this miniature <em>Simon de Varie Kneeling in Prayer</em>, assigns it to Jean Fouquet, and dates it to 1455. The leaf has its own accession, 85.ML.27.2. Retain both the parent manuscript and the leaf location. The date is the museum’s dating of the work; it does not automatically date every intervention now visible on its surface. <a href="https://www.getty.edu/art/collection/object/103S0J">[1]</a></p>
      <p>A folio counts a leaf. A page counts a side or, in a digital file, a position in the file’s sequence. A manuscript’s folio 87 therefore need not be the eighty-seventh page of a presentation PDF. Our Portuguese exercise makes that difference concrete: the archive places its illustration of book 19, folio 87 on PDF page 23. Keep the archival location and the digital navigation aid in separate fields. If you later find a full-volume scan, the PDF page number may cease to help; the archival locator remains the route back to the witness.</p>
      <p>Likewise, preserve the institution’s actual recto or verso notation when it supplies one. The French record used here says folio 2. Do not add a side designation simply because it seems likely. A more precise identifier is helpful only when its precision comes from evidence. Record the form of access, the date consulted, and the selected inscription’s position on the leaf. “Lower scroll” is already more useful than “the motto.”</p>
      <h2 id="french">Two scrolls, three different texts</h2>
      <p>Open the <a href="/transcription-desk/?witness=simon-de-varie-2">French witness at the desk</a>. Before revealing the reference reading, inspect the lower scroll. It has red lettering on a gold-colored band, curved around a fold. Decorative marks occupy space alongside the letters. The upper scroll presents a second inscription. The task is small enough to be manageable and complicated enough to reward attention.</p>
      <p>Make a first pass describing the lettering’s arrangement. Where does a sequence begin and end? Which marks look alphabetic? Which may be separators? Which strokes occupy a shared space? A large painted capital can have decorative features that a modern font cannot reproduce. You are establishing what your transcription will represent, rather than assuming the word processor can preserve the whole object.</p>
      <p>A <strong>diplomatic transcription</strong> records a chosen level of the witness’s written form under an explicit editorial policy. There is no universal switch labeled “perfect diplomatic.” One project may preserve line breaks, letter case, and abbreviation marks; another may treat decorative capitals consistently as ordinary letters. Explain what you are doing. If you represent joined letter forms as separate characters, say where that occurs and why. If a letter remains doubtful, give it a location and an apparatus entry.</p>
      <p>A <strong>normalized reading</strong> answers a different need. It can supply ordinary word spaces, regularize case, resolve an abbreviation, or use modern accents. Those changes may make the French easier to understand. They should be recoverable from your edition. Normalization is not a warrant to repair every unfamiliar form into the phrase you expected to find.</p>
      <p>A <strong>translation</strong> then carries the established reading into another language. The English translator does not get to settle a doubtful French letter merely by choosing the English sentence that sounds nicest. If an alternative reading would produce an alternative meaning, preserve that relationship in the note. A translated uncertainty is still an uncertainty.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Three editorial layers"><table><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Typical intervention to disclose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Diplomatic</th><td>What written forms am I representing?</td><td>Joined letters, doubtful strokes, separators, line arrangement</td></tr><tr><th>Normalized</th><td>How am I making that reading legible as words?</td><td>Spaces, case, accents, abbreviation expansions</td></tr><tr><th>Translated</th><td>How do I convey the reading’s meaning in English?</td><td>Pronoun choices, idiom, retained ambiguity</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
      <p>Getty’s exhibition identifies the two phrases as personal mottoes and supplies modern French readings. Its reference to desire contains a first-person possessive; a working English rendering should explain whether it keeps that personal voice or generalizes it. Compare “life to my liking” with a more literal construction about life according to my desire. These are editorial alternatives to evaluate, not rival discoveries of a new inscription. <a href="https://www.getty.edu/art/exhibitions/power_piety/">[2]</a></p>
      <p>The related <a href="https://www.getty.edu/art/collection/object/103S0E">folio 1 record</a> reports joined A and M forms and connects the desire phrase with an anagram of Simon de Varie’s name. This is a useful lead, but it concerns a separately located leaf. Record the reference as a comparison, then inspect what is present on folio 2. A statement about one opening should not quietly become your observation of another.</p>
      <p>You can test an anagram by comparing letter counts after stating your normalization rules. Removing spaces and treating accents consistently is a declared operation. A successful count shows that two strings share the relevant letters under those rules. It does not independently prove the original writer’s intention, identify the painter, or establish the history of the paint. Keep the modest result and the museum’s interpretation in their respective columns.</p>
      <h2 id="layers">A manuscript date is not a paint-layer date</h2>
      <p>The arms around the French miniature are part of the evidence, even when your immediate task is lettering. The photograph shows interrupted surfaces and armorial forms in several positions. Getty reports partial overpainting of the border arms on folio 2. The folio 1 record separately describes a seventeenth-century replacement with Bourbon-Condé arms. Those catalogue statements help frame a material history; they are not a conservation investigation carried out by this project. <a href="https://www.getty.edu/art/collection/object/103S0J">[1]</a> <a href="https://www.getty.edu/art/collection/object/103S0E">[3]</a></p>
      <p>Suppose you copy the most legible shield into a database, attach “1455,” and identify its bearer from the modern appearance. Your entry could look beautifully tidy while combining evidence from different periods. Add separate fields for the manuscript’s catalogue date, the particular painted display, any reported intervention, and the source of each interpretation. Leave the date of an unexamined layer unresolved.</p>
      <p>Resist two opposite shortcuts. Do not treat the whole leaf as an untouched image of its original commission. Do not dismiss it as worthless because it changed. An altered object can document later ownership, collecting, representation, or appropriation, provided the relevant intervention can be dated and interpreted. Its usefulness depends on the question and the evidence available.</p>
      <p>Before reconstructing earlier arms, ask what would support the reconstruction: an unaltered parallel, a historical description, technical imaging, or a conservation account. A reconstruction should state which features come from which witness. A clean teaching drawing can explain a hypothesis, but it cannot replace the material record of how that hypothesis was reached.</p>
      <h2 id="portuguese">The Portuguese caption is a witness of its own</h2>
      <p>The Torre do Tombo’s presentation on Vasco da Gama provides a sharply located Iberian exercise. On PDF page 23 it identifies the <em>Livro do Armeiro-Mor</em>, 1509, and cites TT, Casa Real, Cartório da Nobreza, book 19, folio 87. Its modern caption quotes the identification “Dom Vasco da Gama almirante da Índia.” This desk verifies that caption and locator; it does not publish them as an independently checked transcription of the manuscript hand. <a href="https://antt.dglab.gov.pt/wp-content/uploads/sites/17/2025/02/vasco-da-gama-conde-almirante.pdf#page=23">[4]</a></p>
      <p>Open the <a href="/transcription-desk/?witness=armeiro-mor-87">Portuguese witness</a> and the archive’s PDF in adjacent tabs or windows. First record the caption as a modern archival statement. Then choose a short span of the actual manuscript lettering. State what you can inspect at the available resolution. If the archive file cannot be opened, mark that limitation: you can still analyze the caption’s vocabulary, but you have not read the manuscript.</p>
      <p>In an English working note, distinguish the personal designation, the office term, and the geographical expression. A convenient modern paraphrase can make historical wording seem more familiar while hiding its institutional setting. Retaining <em>almirante</em> at first occurrence with a gloss lets you discuss the office without pretending that a short caption supplies its full legal history.</p>
      <p>Now compare the two witnesses. Does the modern caption preserve the manuscript’s spelling? Has it supplied accents, word spaces, or capitalization? Has it silently expanded a form? These are questions to investigate, not changes we have already established. Record each demonstrated difference. If the evidence is insufficient to establish a difference, leave the comparison provisional.</p>
      <p>The facsimile remains on the archive’s website because its reproduction terms have not been verified for this project. That affects how we display the witness; it does not require abandoning the scholarly exercise. Cite the exact location, link the institutional source, and keep your observations distinct from reproduced images. The French image has a separately verified CC0 declaration in its IIIF manifest. An open license at one institution does not establish the terms at another. <a href="https://media.getty.edu/iiif/manifest/3/55d52612-3142-481c-8f92-95ee387c29b2">[5]</a></p>
      <h2 id="apparatus">Make uncertainty useful</h2>
      <p>A critical apparatus explains a reading at the place where a reader might need to challenge it. Give the location, the observed form, your proposed expansion or alternative, your review status, and the evidence. “Uncertain” communicates a feeling. “Upper scroll, joined form between the second and fourth groups; resolved from a parallel on another leaf, not from this photograph alone” communicates a reproducible argument.</p>
      <p>The desk uses three statuses. <strong>Unresolved</strong> leaves the decision open. <strong>Provisional</strong> records a working proposal still needing a defined check. <strong>Checked</strong> records that you have performed a check; it does not imply the archive, the museum, or a specialist approved the reading. Name the reviewer or the comparison and explain what it established. A checked reading can still be revised.</p>
      <p>Use a consistent notation policy. You might choose square brackets for supplied text, a question mark for a doubtful reading, and an explicit phrase for an unreadable span whose extent is unknown. Those are possible local conventions, not a universal standard imposed here. Define yours before using them, especially when brackets could otherwise be mistaken for characters actually present in the source.</p>
      <p>Export Markdown when another reader needs a legible report. Export JSON when you want to restore the draft elsewhere. Both retain the witness location and the apparatus; the JSON preserves distinct fields for reloading. Import previews the proposed draft before replacement and uses this desk’s verified source metadata. Keep the earlier export if a revision changes your argument. Browser autosave is convenient, but a cleared browser should not erase the history of your edition.</p>
      <h2 id="assignment">A short edition with a long enough trail</h2>
      <p><strong>Assignment:</strong> Submit a 900–1,200-word editorial report and a desk export for one short inscription. Include a witness inventory, your transcription policy, diplomatic and normalized readings, a working translation, at least two apparatus entries, and a conclusion about what the evidence can support. If the image is inaccessible, submit a caption study that explicitly limits its claims to that modern witness.</p>
      <p>Then add a 250-word comparison with the other dossier. Concentrate on the relation between the original object and the modern description. The aim is not to declare which country has better heraldry. It is to show how a source’s form and mediation change the claims you can make.</p>
      <table><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Points</th><th>Evidence of a strong submission</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Witness control</th><td>25</td><td>Correct locator, access form, separate source voices and dates</td></tr><tr><th>Editorial policy and reading</th><td>25</td><td>Declared conventions, reproducible text, disclosed interventions</td></tr><tr><th>Apparatus and translation</th><td>30</td><td>Located doubts, reasoned alternatives, meaning tied to the established reading</td></tr><tr><th>Argument and rights</th><td>20</td><td>Proportionate conclusion, image status, concrete next check</td></tr></tbody></table>
      <details><summary>What would a defensible conclusion sound like?</summary><p>“I inspected the lower scroll in the Getty image and compared my reading with the museum’s reference. My normalization supplies word spaces and regularizes case under the policy above. One joined form remains provisional pending comparison with another located leaf. The manuscript’s catalogue date does not independently date the overpainted armorial forms.” That conclusion makes real progress and gives the next reader something specific to check.</p><p>For the Portuguese dossier, a caption-only conclusion should say that the modern archive identifies the display with Vasco da Gama at the cited location. It should also say that the manuscript hand has not yet been independently read. The missing check is part of the result.</p></details>
      <p><a class="btn btn-dark" href="/transcription-desk/">Open the transcription desk ↗</a></p>
      <h2>Continue with another located occurrence</h2><p>The desk now includes Getty folio 2v from the same manuscript. Bring your single-witness draft into the <a href="/transcription-desk/compare/">comparison bench</a>, select a second occurrence, and record the relation between the two sources before interpreting agreement. The <a href="/transcription-desk/collation/">collation workshop</a> explains exact-text highlighting, editorial layers, located comparisons, and the difference between recurrence in one book and independent confirmation from another copy.</p>
      <h2>Sources and further work</h2><ol class="sources"><li><a href="https://www.getty.edu/art/collection/object/103S0J">Getty, <em>Simon de Varie Kneeling in Prayer</em></a>, Ms. 7, fol. 2, accession 85.ML.27.2. Modern collection metadata and interpretation; consulted 9 October 2026.</li><li><a href="https://www.getty.edu/art/exhibitions/power_piety/">Getty, <em>Faces of Power and Piety</em></a>, Simon de Varie section. Institutional reference readings of the mottoes; consulted 9 October 2026.</li><li><a href="https://www.getty.edu/art/collection/object/103S0E">Getty, <em>Coat of Arms Held by a Woman</em></a>, Ms. 7, fol. 1, accession 85.ML.27.1. Separate folio comparison; consulted 9 October 2026.</li><li><a href="https://antt.dglab.gov.pt/wp-content/uploads/sites/17/2025/02/vasco-da-gama-conde-almirante.pdf#page=23">Torre do Tombo, archival presentation on Vasco da Gama</a>, PDF p. 23, <em>Livro do Armeiro-Mor</em>, liv. 19, f. 87. Portuguese modern caption verified; original-hand transcription remains open.</li><li><a href="https://media.getty.edu/iiif/manifest/3/55d52612-3142-481c-8f92-95ee387c29b2">Getty’s IIIF manifest for Ms. 7</a>, canvas labeled fol. 2. Image CC0; catalogue prose separately CC BY 4.0. Local JPEG is a compressed full-leaf reproduction, without cropping or restoration.</li></ol>
      <p>Continue with the <a href="/seminars/02-philology-and-translation/">translation seminar</a>, the <a href="/traditions/france/">French tradition study</a>, the <a href="/traditions/portugal/">Portuguese tradition study</a>, or the Academy’s <a href="/academy/reading-the-evidence/">documentary capstone</a>.</p>
    </div>
